{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive play against other players, including repeatedly facing specific cards and needing to defend against them. It also explicitly describes a pay-to-win structure in which skill alone is insufficient to progress or rank higher, implying that spending money provides an in-game advantage. The complaint about annoying strategies does not by itself establish encouragement of dishonest or harmful behavior, and card buffs do not establish power creep because the review does not say purchased items become less valuable over time.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game makes the player compete against other players, with the review describing the opponents and cards encountered in that competition."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly characterizes the game as pay to win and says skill alone does not allow the player to progress to a higher position, implying that spending provides an advantage."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several game elements but only two match coded mechanics. The game lets players trade lives with friends or teammates, which is a social resource-sharing mechanic covered by Reciprocity. The reviewer also refers to purchasing “the pass,” indicating a battle-pass-style paid progression product, which is covered by Recurring Fee. The mention of 9 diamonds does not by itself establish real-money currency exchange, and the move limit describes a game condition but not a scheduled play requirement, waiting timer, or grinding mechanic. The statements that players do not have to pay to win and that customer service failed are evaluations or service complaints, not additional mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes [indicators] under M_RecurringFee",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly identifies a purchasable pass, which is a battle-pass product covered by Recurring Fee."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Indicator: feeling obligated to return a favor, share resources, trade an item, etc.",
      "rationale": "The game supports exchanging the life resource with other players, matching the resource-sharing or trading indicator."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three currently present mechanics despite phrasing some points as suggestions. New lives are gated by an in-game waiting period, so the player cannot immediately continue and wants the timer reduced. The game provides daily prizes that reset when a player misses a day, which both encourages daily return and penalizes absence. The reviewer also mentions small tasks such as celebrating, but does not clearly establish that these tasks are repetitive progress requirements, so Grinding is not assigned. The statements that levels get harder and that the game is addictive do not independently identify a coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.",
      "rationale": "The phrase implies that continuing play requires waiting for a life to regenerate under the game's timer."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars / darkpattern.games definition: encourages return visits every day and punishes missing a day.",
      "rationale": "Daily prizes are provided on a daily schedule and reset when the player fails to log in."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally: there is no visible close control, tapping the ad screen redirects to a store, returning to the game leaves the ad stuck, and the player must terminate the app to escape. This explicitly describes compelled ad viewing and a UI designed to remove or obscure the opt-out path. The store redirection is part of the ad interaction, but the review does not clearly say that an in-game purchase is completed or that the game's purchase interface itself reduces spending friction, so M_EasyToPurchase is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "definition; compelled to view advertisements",
      "rationale": "The player is forced to remain in the advertisement because it provides no way to close it normally."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; UI designed to focus attention away from opportunities to opt out",
      "rationale": "The ad omits the expected dismissal control and makes screen taps redirect to the store, obscuring or removing the opt-out path."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an existing consequence of leaving the game: deleting it would forfeit their in-game purchases and accumulated progress. Those prior investments make uninstalling costly, which fits Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. The request to add progress and purchase saving is hypothetical and is not coded as a separate mechanic. The review does not establish a gameplay save restriction or a requirement to continue a session, so Mandatory Marathon does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: having invested time and money into the game, it is challenging to abandon those gains.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer says uninstalling would forfeit purchases and accumulated work, making departure costly because of value already invested."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes building and expanding an empire and battling other players in real-time combat. The empire-building is a general gameplay activity without a coded dark-pattern mechanic, while real-time battles explicitly place players in competition with one another. No monetization, retention schedule, repetitive task, social obligation, or technical deception is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the game making players compete against other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an energy limitation at level 113 and says purchasing is necessary to have any chance of progressing. This explicitly makes game progress contingent on spending money, satisfying Pay to Progress. The review does not describe a timer, daily reset, advertisement, or a specific currency exchange mechanism.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that purchasing is necessary to make progress in the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an energy system that stops progress when energy is depleted, followed by a relatively long recovery timer before the player can continue. This is an in-game timer imposing a wait to resume gameplay. The requests for console play, a storyline, bigger maps, and more customization are hypothetical additions and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make the player arbitrarily wait before continuing.",
      "rationale": "Depleted energy blocks further progress until the game-controlled recovery timer restores it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes the game using forced notifications to request permission to access the player's contact list. This explicitly describes the game seeking access to the player's social graph, which supports Friend Spam / Impersonation even though the review does not state that messages were actually sent to contacts. The statements about uninstalling and calling it a spam app are evaluations, not separate mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "Forced notifications to give you permission to my contact list",
      "rule_applied": "Indicator: Game uses access to the player’s contact list / social graph.",
      "rationale": "The game is described as using notifications to obtain permission to access the player's contact list, which is access to the player's social graph."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progress being blocked by difficult levels and by tasks they say are impossible without purchasing boosters. The statement that passing hard levels takes a week indicates prolonged difficulty, but it does not explicitly describe repetitive tasks, timers, or a required schedule, so it does not support Grinding or Playing by Appointment. Buying boosters is presented as necessary to overcome the tasks and continue progressing, which supports Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Plus there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review states that completing in-game tasks is impossible unless the player purchases boosters, making payment necessary for progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event with a reward nearly reached: they had only three steps remaining toward 525 dice but could not continue after running out of dice. The event displayed two days remaining, yet after the reviewer stopped to sleep and logged back in, the event had changed. This makes the game’s event schedule determine when the player must participate and creates pressure that stopping will cause a missed reward. The review does not explicitly describe purchasing dice, watching ads, daily rewards, or repetitive grinding, so those are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The event schedule determines the available play window, and the event changed while the player was away despite showing two days remaining."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The nearly completed reward and lack of dice create pressure to keep playing so the player does not miss the event reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an invitation-based social reward mechanic: the player is expected to invite friends and get them to join Facebook in exchange for rewards. This matches Forced Fellowship's social recruitment indicator even though the reviewer says the rewards have stopped. The difficulty obtaining dice is resource scarcity but does not, by itself, identify a listed pattern. The references to max bet, luck, Shut downs, and bank heists describe chance-based gameplay, but no real-money participation is stated, so M_Gambling is not supported. Duplicate stickers do not explicitly state a compulsion to complete the collection. The inability to log in is attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical malfunction and does not trigger a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: patterns that reward players for inviting friends or encourage/force invitations to gain in-game incentives.",
      "rationale": "The span describes an existing social-recruitment mechanic in which inviting friends and bringing them into the game is tied to rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a default-selection interface behavior: opening the game or entering the 1v1 section automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet table available for the player's coin balance. This steers players toward the most expensive wager and creates a risk of unintended high-stakes play. The review also says that losing coins leads players to buy more coins with real money, but it does not clearly state that payment is required for progress, so M_PayToProgress and M_IntermediateCurrency are not assigned. The mention of a 1v1 section identifies player-versus-player competition, but it is contextual rather than a described pressure or manipulation mechanic, so S_Competition is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically directs attention and selection toward the highest-stakes option instead of a neutral or low-bet starting choice."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes increasingly unfavorable random outcomes, saying the RNG is against them, but does not establish that the game hides information about skill or uses chance-based rewards purchased with real money. They also describe a progression economy in which playing without spending actual money is unrewarding and a level costs 2200 while the victory reward barely replenishes that cost. This implies that continued progress or economically viable play is pressured toward real-money spending, supporting Pay to Progress. The near break-even level cost does not by itself establish Waste Aversion because no leftover premium currency or small currency mismatch is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes progression-oriented play as effectively requiring or pressuring real-money spending to remain worthwhile."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game requiring repetitive progression, explicitly called a grinder. It also describes competitive events where winning is effectively unavailable to ordinary players unless they spend very large amounts of money, including a reported 30k expenditure to win the last event. The event language and pay-to-win claim describe competition between players, while the money-for-advantage mechanic supports Pay to Progress. The spending amount alone is not a separate label, and the review does not describe a specific purchase interface, currency, gambling, or recurring fee.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Same old same old grinder",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Calling the game a grinder describes repetitive and tedious gameplay used for progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes spending money as the route to gaining an advantage and winning."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review describes players competing in events for a win."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several current game mechanics. It explicitly identifies a pay-to-win structure, meaning spending money provides gameplay advantages. Base upgrades use increasingly long update timers, imposing waits before progress can continue. Progress also expands endlessly and requires ongoing repetitive grinding, which supports both Infinite Treadmill and Grinding. The UI is described as intentionally confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases; this is purposeful UI direction away from clear choices and toward buying. The alliance system creates multiplayer conflict and pressure: dominant alliances dictate development choices, while unaffiliated players are attacked and bullied. This supports Competition, but not Forced Fellowship because the review does not describe recruiting friends or an obligation to avoid letting down allies.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes a design where paying players receive gameplay advantages."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits",
      "rationale": "Base progress is delayed by increasingly long game-imposed update timers."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that continually expand the game to never allow players to complete",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling implies that progression continually extends rather than reaching a meaningful completion point."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "definition: performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly characterizes continued progress as requiring more grind."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "definition: UI elements designed to purposefully focus attention on selected options or away from opportunities to opt out",
      "rationale": "The UI is intentionally made confusing and overwhelming to steer players toward purchasing."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that make players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "The alliance system places players in hostile conflict with other players, including attacks against those outside alliances."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that has been changed so the player must first bet 50,000 in order to complete and collect the daily rewards. This is an existing recurring reward task with a condition for claiming it, so it supports Daily Rewards. The review does not establish real-money spending, repetitive grinding, or a specific play time or wait imposed by a timer.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards.",
      "rule_applied": "Daily task lists with resetting rewards; definition of Daily Rewards",
      "rationale": "The game provides daily rewards and requires the player to complete a daily betting requirement to obtain them."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a monetization gate on obtaining wins and completing sticker collections: the reviewer says winning costs hundreds of dollars and that the final stickers cannot be obtained without paying substantially. The sticker statement also describes a collection-completion objective, with the last one or two items serving as the missing pieces. The claim that goals succeed only rarely expresses frustration and low odds, but it does not clearly describe repetitive tasks, gambling, or an impossible-to-complete game mechanic. The statement about becoming addicted is a warning about engagement, not a specific coded mechanic. No web search is needed because the relevant mechanics are explicit in the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Pay Wall definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that achieving wins requires substantial real-money spending, describing payment as necessary for progress or advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "definition; drives players to feel compelled to acquire all in-game items",
      "rationale": "The review describes pursuing the final missing stickers in order to complete a sticker collection."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a level-based puzzle game in which progress has become difficult and slow. The clearest mechanic is that the player must purchase add-ons to get through the later levels, making payment a condition of progress. The limited lives are mentioned as a source of repeated failure and delay, but the review does not explicitly describe a wait timer, daily reward, repetitive task requirement, or a session that cannot be stopped. The statements about wasting money and time describe the player's accumulated investment, but do not by themselves establish a design that makes leaving difficult.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through a sane mind.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly states that purchasing add-ons is necessary to make progress through the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review contrasts misleading external advertisements with the actual game. The advertised physics puzzles are marketing material and are outside the coding scope under R13, so the ads do not trigger a label. In the game itself, the reviewer describes training, building, and gathering resources as chores required to reach the occasional puzzles. This explicitly describes repetitive, tedious tasks being performed to make progress toward desired content, which supports Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "you'll be doing chores in order to get to them",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes tedious tasks that must be completed to make progress toward the puzzles."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two out-of-scope elements: the developer's advertising of pin-pull levels, and a connection problem after required updates. The in-game behavior explicitly described is that the player paid real money for extra coins and other items. This establishes real-world money being used to acquire in-game currency, but the review does not state that payment is required for progress, that purchases are easy or accidental, or that the rare mini-games are deliberately limited by a pricing mechanism. The player's high level and long playtime alone do not establish difficulty leaving, grinding, or an endless game structure.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "I have also paid a lot for extra coins and things",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes spending real-world money to acquire coins used within the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a pay-to-win progression system: the reviewer says progress cannot realistically be made without paying and that the newest bot must be bought rather than earned. The reference to a shifting meta implies that newly introduced bots replace or devalue older options, supporting Power Creep. The game also presents in-game purchase deals every time the player logs in before play, but the review does not clearly describe a purchase-by-default option, accidental purchase risk, insufficient-funds prompt, or attention-directing UI, so M_EasyToPurchase and M_UIMisdirection are not assigned. Finally, free-to-play rewards are obtained through ads, which describes incentivized advertising.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck! Even if you wanted to do f2p, you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay to Win and Pay Wall indicators",
      "rationale": "The review states that progression and keeping up with the game require purchasing the newest bot with real money rather than earning it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The shifting meta and need to buy the newest bot imply that newer purchased bots diminish the usefulness or competitiveness of existing options over time."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rule_applied": "definition; forced or incentivized advertisements",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes free-to-play rewards as being obtained through viewing or engaging with advertisements."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited card-collection set. Card packs stop providing new cards when the player is close to completing the set, especially near the set's expiration, so the player can fail to obtain the completion reward despite having collected 125 cards. This directly creates pressure to complete the collection before the game-defined deadline and fear of missing the reward. The review does not clearly describe repetitive tasks, paid currency, gambling, or a purchase-related scarcity tactic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "after collecting 125 cards from the beginning of the collection you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The mechanic centers on acquiring all cards in a set to obtain a completion reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "especially when the set is almost expiring",
      "rule_applied": "definition: players are pressured by the fear of missing out on rewards if they stop playing or fail to complete an event in time",
      "rationale": "The expiring set creates a risk of missing the completion reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "when the set is almost expiring",
      "rule_applied": "definition: requires players to play according to a game-defined schedule or deadline",
      "rationale": "The set's expiration imposes a game-defined time window in which players must continue pursuing cards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a capped inventory system that limits available board and refrigerator storage. Many spaces are occupied by generator parts, lower-level generators that cannot yet be merged, and high-level food items the player refuses to delete. This storage pressure makes continued play depend on spending money to buy parts, so payment is presented as necessary to keep playing. The reluctance to delete valuable accumulated items also makes abandoning or discarding progress costly. No grinding, daily schedule, competition, advertising, or gambling mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Not enough slots to store and play without spending $$ to buy parts.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review states that continued play is not possible without spending money to obtain parts, making payment a requirement for progress or continued gameplay."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "98 spaces total, 72 are taken up by parts of generators or lower generators that I can't merge with higher generators yet",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory.",
      "rationale": "The game imposes a limited storage capacity that becomes occupied by unusable or not-yet-mergeable items, creating pressure to discard items or spend to overcome the capacity constraint."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "high level items like a corn that I am not going to delete",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value",
      "rationale": "The player’s accumulated high-level items are treated as valuable investments that they are unwilling to discard, making departure or abandoning progress more difficult."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a collection goal involving photos, with the player repeatedly playing bingo rounds to obtain missing items and complete a set. This repeated round-by-round activity is a progression grind. The game also offers a paid shortcut after a lost round: spending extra coins is presented as a way to claim a missing photo and complete the set, so the mechanic is pay-to-progress. The reviewer additionally says the promised photo is not delivered, but this is a failure of the offer rather than a separate codebook mechanic; no label is assigned solely from the complaint about money wasted.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I play round after round to collect 'photos'",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player performs repeated bingo rounds in order to make progress toward collecting the photos."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "collect 'photos', and after each round that I lose it will ask me if I want to spend extra coins to claim a photo I don't have to complete the set",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game centers progress on obtaining missing photos and completing a set, directly invoking the compulsion to complete an in-game collection."
    },
